- op: discuss  # 0de7
  at: '2026-04-20T17:30:10Z'  # 0de7
  by: agent  # 0de7
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0de7
  clock: 7  # 0de7
  nonce: 0de7  # 0de7
  message: "Agreed on both counts. Filed two tracker items so the installer story ships independently of the analyzer scaffold:\n\n- **WI-dotud** (P3, todo_soft): 'hypergumbo install-rust-analyzer' subcommand, analog of install-gitleaks / install-embeddings. --check reports availability; default action installs via 'rustup component add rust-analyzer' when rustup is present (blessed upstream path), with a pre-built-binary download fallback into the XDG cache dir. New is_rust_analyzer_available() helper that both the --check flag and WI-nohah's graceful-degrade fall-through logic will consume. Does NOT depend on WI-duzul landing — the installer is independent infrastructure; WI-duzul Slice C will consume is_rust_analyzer_available() from WI-dotud.\n\n- **WI-huham** (P3, todo_soft, isbefore-after WI-dotud): 'hypergumbo install-extras' umbrella subcommand that runs gitleaks + embeddings + rust-analyzer installers in sequence. --check prints a status table; --only/--skip accept subsets; matching uninstall-extras mirrors the individual uninstall subcommands. Pure orchestration over existing installers, so it lands only after WI-dotud so the umbrella has all three to enumerate.\n\nWI-duzul Slice C stays scoped to analyzer-registry wiring + opt-in flag + Analyzer subclass integration. It will depend on WI-dotud for the availability check but not block on the umbrella — users can run 'hypergumbo install-rust-analyzer' directly before enabling the backend.\n\nWiring order this implies for session planning:\n1. WI-dotud (install-rust-analyzer) — can land before or after WI-duzul Slice C; no hard dependency either way.\n2. WI-duzul Slice C (analyzer wiring + opt-in flag) — consumes is_rust_analyzer_available from WI-dotud if that landed; otherwise Slice C ships with its own inline shutil.which check and WI-dotud's helper replaces it later.\n3. WI-nohah (graceful-degrade fall-through) — natural next step after the registry wiring exists.\n4. WI-huham (install-extras umbrella) — once WI-dotud is in, fold it in."  # 0de7
- op: discuss  # 9a6e
  at: '2026-04-20T20:28:42Z'  # 9a6e
  by: agent  # 9a6e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9a6e
  clock: 8  # 9a6e
  nonce: 9a6e  # 9a6e
  message: "Opt-in gate shipped as PR #3242 (merged to dev as 11db63fcf). new hypergumbo_lang_rust_analyzer.gate.should_use_rust_analyzer_backend chains the HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER env var / --backend rust-analyzer flag with is_rust_analyzer_available from WI-dotud; 26 tests at 100% coverage.\n\n**Remaining Slice C work requires an analyzer-registry architecture decision.** Investigation finding: hypergumbo_core.analyze.registry.register_analyzer is keyed by name (e.g. 'rust'). Two analyzers cannot both claim 'rust' and have the registry arbitrate between them — each registered analyzer just runs when asked. Options for how Slice C should activate the backend:\n\n1. **Register a new analyzer name 'rust_analyzer'** that processes .rs files only when should_use_rust_analyzer_backend returns True (else returns an empty AnalysisResult). Both 'rust' and 'rust_analyzer' would run per file; downstream dedup (via stable_id parity from WI-bajuz) would merge outputs. Pros: no registry change. Cons: doubles the .rs walk when the backend is active.\n\n2. **Add a priority-based supersede mechanism** to the registry: higher-priority analyzer's output for a given language supersedes lower. Pros: clean dispatch. Cons: structural change to registry that touches every analyzer's execution path; needs careful migration.\n\n3. **Monkey-patch hypergumbo_lang_mainstream.rust.analyze_rust** at import time in the rust_analyzer package to delegate: if gate=True, call SCIP backend; else fall through to the original. Pros: minimal registry change. Cons: hidden import-order dependency; fragile under entry-point discovery.\n\nMy recommendation is option 1 for the first implementation — it keeps the registry untouched and the duplicate-walk cost only bites when the user explicitly opted in (already the slow path per WI-zakub). Stable_id parity ensures dedup works. Option 2 is a cleaner endgame but needs its own tracker item with careful scope.\n\nAll primitives needed for option 1 are in place: gate (this slice), try_analyze_with_rust_analyzer (WI-nohah), translate_scip_to_hg (Slice A), run_rust_analyzer_scip (Slice B-first), is_rust_analyzer_available (WI-dotud). Slice C-final is now pure wiring: @register_analyzer('rust_analyzer', priority=45) returning AnalysisResult(symbols=[], edges=[]) when gate=False else chaining invoke+translate and setting symbol.origin='scip' as the provenance marker from the WI description. Recommend scoping that as the closing slice next session."  # 9a6e
